cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code or these results, please cite it as below. A preprint is in preparation; this file will be updated with its reference."
title: "The skribe: a rule-based, meaningless generator that reproduces the statistics of the Voynich manuscript text"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Quadrada
    given-names: Quim
repository-code: "https://github.com/quimqu/voynich-scribe"
license: MIT
version: 0.2.0
date-released: 2026-10-26
keywords:
  - Voynich manuscript
  - text generation
  - self-citation
  - statistical analysis
abstract: >-
  A seven-step writing procedure with no meaning anywhere (sheet habits, copying from the
  visible page, a two-glyph memory, a boundary rule between words, line start and end forms,
  paragraph rules) writes a complete fake Voynich manuscript on the real layout. Measured
  against the Zandbergen-Landini transliteration (ZL3b) with a battery of 78 statistics, it
  reproduces 70; learned from the odd folios only, it writes the even ones at 64 of 78 (the
  real halves agree on 69). It also passes the four boundary signatures of Parisel (2026) for
  both Currier dialects, and reproduces the page-similarity pattern that Layfield and Davis
  (2026) read as evidence of misbinding.
